On 2007-07-24, Gary Olson <garyolson@pobox.NOSPAM.com> wrote:
> Derek Ray wrote:
>> Previously, for a skilled player, the solution was "Burn E, kill all
>> three in melee, move on 20 turns later as if nothing happened."  The 
>> no-brainer's gone... but that doesn't mean there aren't several other 
>> solutions available.
>> 
> Not everyone plays in this fashion. Although E is part of the game, I avoid the E word as a test of 
> skill (except for Demogorgon -- he's just plain rude).

Unfortunately, this is effectively a conduct: "Elberethless".  While
I certainly understand the desire to avoid using the E-word where
possible, I can't balance the game for conducts; I have to assume that
players will aggressively try to win using any and all means necessary.

Also unfortunately, in vanilla one of _the_ most effective no-brainer
solutions to a problem is "Engrave Elbereth".  It's even super-useful to
clear a path for yourself on the ascension run back up, as monsters will
begin fleeing from you -- thus requiring you not spend time having
to kill them just to get them out of your way.

> I would like to suggest you give E a "half-life" -- each successive use of E reduces its 
> effectiveness on a non-linear scale. The first use is guaranteed; further uses are less effective.

This makes the game randomly easier for players who didn't need to use
any dust-E in the early-game due to the occasional kindness of the RNG.
This renders it really no less imbalanced; it's just a nuisance for the
weaker early-game classes, who now get a harder late-game as well due to
E being unavailable, while the Valk and Barb who smash their way past 
the early game effortlessly can shrug off the summon-nasties later as
well.

I appreciate the input, but I think I'd prefer to give this particular
change a great deal more time in playtest before trying to see if 
it needs changing.  So far I've gotten reasonably good feedback; the
only issues I've heard so far have been from people who mistakenly 
assumed that "reduced effectiveness" meant "zero effectiveness" and 
just refused to use it at all for fear that it would fail.  The curve
isn't _that_ sharp; an early game character can make quite effective use
of the word still, and sufficiently low-level beasties still respect it
at 100%.  Other than that, people have been able to use it as exactly what
I intended; a certain degree of protection, but not a near-infinite
bulletproof shield.

